\title{EML Differential Equations: A Differential-Algebra Framework for
Riccati Reduction, Wronskian Existence Criteria, and the Kovacic Parity
Obstruction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, entirely within the algebra of differential fields, the structural
backbone of the closed-form solution theory for second-order linear ordinary
differential equations $y'' = a\,y$ whose coefficients lie in a field of
Exponential--Logarithmic (EML) functions. Three pillars are established.
\emph{(1) Canonical form.} The logarithmic-derivative substitution $v = y'/y$
(the Riccati transform) reduces $y'' = a\,y$ to the first-order quadratic Riccati
equation $v' + v^2 = a$ in any differential field, with no analytic,
characteristic, or algebraic-closure hypotheses. \emph{(2) Existence criteria.}
The field of constants $\Const = \{x : x' = 0\}$ is the base field over which the
solution space is a module of dimension at most two; the Wronskian
$W = y_1 y_2' - y_2 y_1'$ of two solutions is always a constant (abstract Abel
identity), and a pair of solutions is a fundamental system exactly when $W$ is a
nonzero constant. \emph{(3) The algebra--geometry gap.} While the algebraic
skeleton is always present, its realization by explicit EML solutions can fail.
We give a sharp, decidable witness: for the polynomial-coefficient family
$y'' = f\,y$, the first step of the Kovacic algorithm (existence of a rational
solution of $v' + v^2 = f$) fails for every odd-degree $f$ and can succeed for
even-degree $f$. Airy's equation $y'' = x\,y$ is the canonical witness: it has
neither a nonzero polynomial solution nor a rational Riccati solution. All
results described here have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The central question of the classical theory of linear ODEs is deceptively
simple: given a second-order linear equation $y'' = a\,y$, can its solutions be
written in \emph{closed form}? Here closed form means an \emph{EML expression}: a
finite formula built from a base field, the independent variable, and the
operations of field arithmetic, root extraction, exponentiation, and logarithm.
Polynomials, $e^{x}$, $\log(1+x^2)$, and $\sqrt{e^{x}+x}$ are EML; the Airy
functions are not.

The modern answer is supplied by differential Galois theory (Picard--Vessiot,
Kolchin) and made algorithmic by Kovacic's algorithm, which decides in finitely
many steps whether $y'' = a\,y$ over a rational-function field admits a
Liouvillian (closed-form) solution. The decision procedure's first step is a
search for a rational solution of the associated Riccati equation.

\subsection*{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A characteristic-free, analysis-free proof that the Riccati transform
        reduces $y'' = a y$ to $v' + v^2 = a$ in any differential field
        (Theorem~\ref{thm:riccati}).
  \item A development of the field of constants as a subfield, the module
        structure of the solution space over it, and the abstract Abel identity
        for the Wronskian (\S\ref{sec:constants}--\S\ref{sec:wronskian}).
  \item A Wronskian-based existence/independence dichotomy: a fundamental system
        is detected precisely by a nonvanishing constant Wronskian
        (Theorem~\ref{thm:fundsys}).
  \item A sharp, two-sided parity obstruction for the Kovacic first step on the
        polynomial-coefficient family, with Airy's equation as canonical
        non-solvable witness and $y'' = (x^2+1)y$ as a solvable witness
        (\S\ref{sec:poly}--\S\ref{sec:kovacic}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{definition}[Differential field]
A \emph{differential field} is a field $K$ with a derivation $' : K \to K$, an
additive map satisfying the Leibniz rule $(fg)' = f'g + fg'$. One derives the
quotient rule $(f/g)' = (f'g - fg')/g^2$ for $g \neq 0$ and the reciprocal rule
$(1/g)' = -g'/g^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[EML field]
A coefficient field is \emph{EML} if obtained from a base differential field by a
finite tower of extensions, each adjoining an element that is algebraic, an
exponential ($u$ with $u'/u \in K$), or a logarithm ($u$ with $u' \in K$). The
prototypical case is $\mathbb{R}(x)$ with $x' = 1$, with differential subring
$\mathbb{R}[x]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic derivative]
For $y \in K$ with $y \neq 0$, $\logDeriv(y) := y'/y$.
\end{definition}

\section{Pillar 1: the Riccati transform}

\begin{theorem}[Riccati transform, raw form]\label{thm:riccati-raw}
For every $y \in K$ with $y \neq 0$,
\[ \logDeriv(y)' + \logDeriv(y)^2 = \frac{y''}{y}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $v = y'/y$. By the quotient rule $v' = y''/y - (y'/y)^2 = y''/y - v^2$, so
$v' + v^2 = y''/y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Riccati transform for $y''=ay$]\label{thm:riccati}
If $y \neq 0$ and $y'' = a\,y$, then $v = y'/y$ satisfies $v' + v^2 = a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute $y'' = ay$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:riccati-raw}: the right-hand side is
$(ay)/y = a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Algebraic normal form]
For $y \neq 0$, $\bigl(\logDeriv(y)' + \logDeriv(y)^2\bigr)\,y = y''$.
\end{theorem}

This substitution is the heart of the Kovacic algorithm: it converts a linear
second-order problem into a nonlinear first-order one whose rational solvability
is decidable by degree analysis.

\section{Pillar 2a: the field of constants and the solution module}
\label{sec:constants}

\begin{definition}[Constants subfield]
$\Const(K) := \{\, x \in K : x' = 0 \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\Const(K)$ is a subfield of $K$; membership is equivalent to $x' = 0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each closure property is a one-line derivation: e.g. $a' = b' = 0$ gives
$(ab)' = 0$ and $(a^{-1})' = -a'/a^2 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First-order solution line]
If $y_1, y_2$ solve $y' = a\,y$ and $y_2 \neq 0$, then $(y_1/y_2)' = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(y_1/y_2)' = (y_1'y_2 - y_1 y_2')/y_2^2 = (ay_1y_2 - y_1 a y_2)/y_2^2 = 0$.
\end{proof}

Hence the solution space of $y' = ay$ is one-dimensional over $\Const(K)$, and its
differential Galois group embeds in $\Const(K)^\times$.

\begin{theorem}[Scaling]
If $c' = 0$ and $y'' = ay$, then $(cy)'' = a(cy)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Superposition]
If $y_1'' = ay_1$ and $y_2'' = ay_2$, then $(y_1+y_2)'' = a(y_1+y_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketches]
$(cy)' = cy'$ since $c'=0$, so $(cy)'' = cy'' = a(cy)$. Additivity gives
$(y_1+y_2)'' = ay_1 + ay_2 = a(y_1+y_2)$.
\end{proof}

Together these make the solution set of $y'' = ay$ a $\Const(K)$-module of
dimension at most two.

\section{Pillar 2b: the Wronskian and fundamental systems}
\label{sec:wronskian}

\begin{definition}[Wronskian]
$W(y_1, y_2) := y_1\,y_2' - y_2\,y_1'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract Abel identity]\label{thm:abel}
If $y_1'' = ay_1$ and $y_2'' = ay_2$, then $W(y_1,y_2)' = 0$; equivalently
$W \in \Const(K)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$W' = y_1 y_2'' - y_2 y_1'' = y_1(ay_2) - y_2(ay_1) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Linear dependence over constants]
$y_1, y_2$ are linearly dependent over $\Const(K)$ if there exist constants
$c_1, c_2$, not both zero, with $c_1 y_1 + c_2 y_2 = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Dependence implies vanishing Wronskian]\label{thm:dep}
If $y_1, y_2$ are linearly dependent over the constants, then $W(y_1, y_2) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $c_1 y_1 + c_2 y_2 = 0$ and $c_i' = 0$ we get $c_1 y_1' + c_2 y_2' = 0$.
Then $c_1 W = 0$ and $c_2 W = 0$; since some $c_i \neq 0$, $W = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonzero Wronskian implies independence]
If $W(y_1, y_2) \neq 0$, then $y_1, y_2$ are linearly independent over the
constants.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental-system criterion]\label{thm:fundsys}
If $y_1'' = ay_1$, $y_2'' = ay_2$, and $W(y_1, y_2) \neq 0$, then $W(y_1,y_2)$ is
a nonzero constant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:abel} with $W \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Pillar 3a: polynomial obstructions}
\label{sec:poly}

We work in $K = \mathbb{R}(x)$ via $\mathbb{R}[x]$; $\degop$ is the natural degree.

\begin{lemma}[Degree mismatch]\label{lem:degmis}
For nonzero $p$, $\degop(p'') < \degop(x\,p)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\degop(xp) = 1 + \degop p$ while $\degop(p'') \le \degop p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Airy has no polynomial solution]\label{thm:airypoly}
There is no nonzero polynomial $p$ with $p'' = x\,p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equality forces $\degop(p'') = \degop(xp)$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:degmis}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[General positive-degree obstruction]
If $\degop q \ge 1$ and $p \neq 0$, then $p'' \neq q\,p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$p'' = qp$ would give $\degop(p'') \ge \degop p + 1$, but
$\degop(p'') \le \degop p - 2$ for nonconstant $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Generalized Airy]
For every $n \ge 1$, $y'' = x^{n} y$ has no nonzero polynomial solution.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial Abel identity]
With $W(f,g) = f g' - g f'$, if $f'' = qf$ and $g'' = qg$ then $W(f,g)' = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Pillar 3b: rational Riccati parity obstruction and Kovacic sharpness}
\label{sec:kovacic}

\begin{definition}[Rational Riccati solvability]
A coefficient $f \in \mathbb{R}[x]$ \emph{has a rational Riccati solution} if
there exist $p, q$ with $q \neq 0$ and
\[ p'\,q - p\,q' + p^2 = f\,q^2. \]
Writing $v = p/q$ this is $v' + v^2 = f$ after clearing denominators.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Wronskian-like degree bound]\label{lem:wbound}
For all $p, q$, $\degop(p'\,q - p\,q') \le \degop p + \degop q - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Odd-degree obstruction]\label{thm:odd}
If $\degop f$ is odd, there are no $p, q$ with $q \neq 0$ and
$p'q - p q' + p^2 = f q^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:wbound} the left side has degree at most
$\max(2\degop p,\ \degop p + \degop q - 1)$, while the right has degree
$\degop f + 2\degop q$. Since $2\degop p$ is even and $\degop f + 2\degop q$ is
odd, the dominant terms cannot match; the Wronskian-like branch is strictly
dominated. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Airy Riccati obstruction]
The Riccati equation $v' + v^2 = x$ has no rational solution.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:odd} with $f = x$, $\degop f = 1$ odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Combined Airy first-step obstruction]
Airy's equation $y'' = xy$ has no nonzero polynomial solution and no rational
solution of its associated Riccati equation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Generalized Airy Riccati obstruction]
For every $k \ge 0$, $f = x^{2k+1}$ has no rational Riccati solution.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[Even-degree solvable witness]\label{thm:even}
The coefficient $f = x^2 + 1$ has the polynomial Riccati solution $v = x$
($p = x$, $q = 1$).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$p'q - pq' + p^2 = 1 + x^2 = f q^2$; equivalently $v = x$ solves
$v' + v^2 = 1 + x^2$.
\end{proof}

This $v = x$ corresponds to $y = e^{x^2/2}$, whose logarithmic derivative is $x$.
Moreover $\degop(x^2 + 1) = 2$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp two-sided parity decision]
On the monomial-coefficient family, every $f = x^{2k+1}$ is obstructed, while
$f = x^2 + 1$ admits a rational Riccati solution; the odd-degree test is a correct
and tight decision rule.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Kovacic first step as an algorithm}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Input a polynomial coefficient $f$ and compute $d = \degop f$.
  \item If $d$ is odd, return ``no rational Riccati solution'' (Theorem~\ref{thm:odd}).
  \item If $d$ is even, search for a polynomial $v$ of degree $d/2$ solving
        $v' + v^2 = f$ by coefficient matching; return $v$ and
        $y = \exp\!\int v$ if found.
\end{enumerate}
Step 2 is $O(1)$; step 4 solves a triangular system in $d/2 + 1$ unknowns,
polynomial in $d$.

\section{Applications}

The framework yields a formula-free proof that Airy functions are not elementary;
a fast parity pre-filter for computer-algebra implementations of Kovacic's
algorithm; a certification that caustics and quantum turning regions have no
elementary closed form; and a verified pedagogical foundation for the constants
field, solution module, and Wronskian criterion.

\section{Discussion}

The algebraic data of a second-order linear EML equation---the constants field,
the solution module of dimension $\le 2$, the Wronskian-detected non-degeneracy,
and the Galois action---is always present. What can fail is its realization by
explicit EML formulas. The Riccati transform translates realization into rational
solvability of $v' + v^2 = a$, and the parity obstruction shows this fails
decidably and sharply. Airy's equation is the minimal canonical witness.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact image characterization]
$f$ admits a polynomial Riccati solution iff $f \in \{ g' + g^2 : g \in
\mathbb{R}[x] \}$, a set meeting every even degree $2n$ ($n \ge 1$) and no odd
degree; on the image the solution is unique up to the involution fixing the
quadratic term.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Rank-2 reduction of order]
If $y_1 \neq 0$ solves $y'' = ay$, a second solution $y_2$ is independent over the
constants iff $W(y_1,y_2)$ is a nonzero constant, and every such $y_2 = y_1 u$
with $u' = W/y_1^2$. Hence the solution space is at most two-dimensional.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree-graded sharpness]
For $f = c\,x^k$ ($c \neq 0$), the Kovacic first step succeeds iff $k$ is even,
and then a rational Riccati solution exists as a polynomial of degree $k/2$;
solution complexity is linear in the degree.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a rigorous, hypothesis-minimal differential-algebra account of the
closed-form solution theory for second-order linear EML equations: the Riccati
transform as canonical form, the constants field and Wronskian as the existence
and non-degeneracy machinery, and a sharp parity obstruction certifying---via
Airy's equation---the gap between an ever-present algebraic structure and a
sometimes-absent explicit formula.

\end{document}

